## 2. Using Set Theory & Combinatorics to Find Supply Chain Cracks
Modern software packages rely on complex matrices of external libraries. You can use Set Theory to map out intersections and dependencies to find forgotten or unmonitored attack vectors.
## How to Hunt with it:

   1. Define the Dependency Sets: Let Set $A$ be the main libraries the website explicitly imports. Let Set $B$ be the hidden sub-libraries that those main libraries import automatically (transitive dependencies).
   2. Look for the Null Intersections ($\emptyset$): Standard automated scanners only flag libraries with known vulnerabilities (CVEs). A math-focused researcher uses combinatorics to analyze combinations of completely safe libraries.
   3. The Invisible Flaw: Sometimes, Library $X$ and Library $Y$ are 100% secure when run independently. However, if both are loaded into the server's global runtime environment simultaneously, their combined variable spaces intersect ($X \cap Y$). If Library $X$ defines a global configuration value that safely overrides a parameter inside Library $Y$, it can unintentionally disable a security constraint inside Library $Y$, opening up a zero-day structural vulnerability.

------------------------------
## 3. Using Boolean Algebra to Bypass Conditionals
Every authorization check on a backend server relies on Boolean logic expressions to grant or deny access, such as:
$$\text{Allowed} = (\text{IsAdmin} \lor \text{IsOwner}) \land \lnot \text{IsAccountSuspended}$$ 
## How to Hunt with it:

   1. Analyze the Truth Tables: When testing an API, look at the error messages to piece together the underlying logic gates the developer wrote.
   2. Apply De Morgan’s Laws: De Morgan's laws state that $\lnot(A \land B) \iff (\lnot A \lor \lnot B)$. Developers often make logical mistakes when writing complex negative conditions (e.g., trying to block users who are neither verified nor local).
   3. The Invisible Flaw: By feeding the API strange parameter combinations (like passing an array ["user", "admin"] instead of a single string text parameter, or passing multiple contrasting authorization headers at the same time), you can force the server's logical evaluations to trigger an unexpected True state due to weak boolean handling.

